    <h1>James Dyson Award Proposal: The "Triage-Link" System</h1>

    <h2>1. The Problem</h2>
    <p>Emergency Departments, such as Surrey Memorial, are experiencing severe "Access Block." Patients often wait 8 to 12 hours in unmonitored waiting rooms before seeing a physician. During this time, triage nurses are overwhelmed and cannot continuously monitor every individual. If a patient's condition rapidly deteriorates (e.g., dropping oxygen levels, sudden cardiac distress, or a physical fall), it often goes unnoticed until it becomes a critical emergency, increasing mortality rates and hospital liability.</p>

    <h2>2. The Idea</h2>
    <div class="highlight">
        <p><strong>The Triage-Link Badge</strong> is a low-cost, reusable, non-invasive wearable device handed to patients upon ER check-in. It creates a "Virtual Ward" out of the waiting room.</p>
    </div>
    <p>Using embedded sensors and an ESP32 microcontroller, the badge continuously monitors heart rate, blood oxygen saturation (SpO2), and patient orientation (fall detection). Rather than triggering loud alarms, the device processes the data locally and uses a wireless protocol (ESP-NOW or Wi-Fi) to update a centralized dashboard at the nurse's station. If vitals cross a critical threshold, the system silently flags the patient, enabling immediate intervention.</p>

