    <h2>4. Implementation Procedure</h2>
    <h3>Phase 1: Breadboard Proof of Concept (The Brain)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Wire the MAX30102 and MPU-6050 sensors to the ESP32 via the I2C bus (SDA/SCL pins).</li>
        <li>Write firmware in C++ (Arduino IDE/PlatformIO) to initialize sensors and read raw values.</li>
        <li>Implement basic DSP (Digital Signal Processing) to calculate a stable BPM and SpO2 percentage from the optical noise.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Phase 2: Connectivity & Logic (The Bridge)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Configure the ESP32 to connect to a local Wi-Fi network or utilize ESP-NOW for peer-to-peer communication to a "master" receiver.</li>
        <li>Write threshold logic: <code>if (SpO2 < 90 || BPM > 120) { triggerAlert(); }</code></li>
        <li>Set up a simple MQTT broker (like Mosquitto) or a web dashboard (Node-RED / Blynk) to visualize the incoming telemetry in real-time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Phase 3: Hardware Miniaturization (The Form Factor)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Transition the circuit from a breadboard to a soldered perf-board, keeping the layout as compact as possible.</li>
        <li>Design a custom PCB layout if time permits, routing the I2C traces carefully to avoid noise.</li>
        <li>Integrate the TP4056 charging circuit and LiPo battery.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Phase 4: Enclosure & Testing (The Dyson Finish)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Design a CAD model for the enclosure. Focus on a "clip-on" mechanism that keeps the MAX30102 flush against the skin (collarbone or wrist).</li>
        <li>3D print the housing using PETG or a smooth material that simulates medical-grade, easily sanitizable plastics.</li>
        <li>Conduct iterative testing: measure the signal degradation when the user walks vs. sits, and refine the software filters accordingly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. Estimated Time Investment</h2>
    <p>This is designed to be a rapid prototype for an engineering team familiar with microcontrollers.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Research & Component Sourcing:</strong> 5 hours</li>
        <li><strong>Phase 1 (Breadboarding & Sensor Code):</strong> 10 - 15 hours</li>
        <li><strong>Phase 2 (Wireless Comms & Dashboard):</strong> 15 hours</li>
        <li><strong>Phase 3 (Soldering/PCB & Power Management):</strong> 10 hours</li>
        <li><strong>Phase 4 (3D Modeling & Physical Testing):</strong> 15 hours</li>
        <li><strong>Total Estimated Time:</strong> ~55 - 60 hours</li>
    </ul>

